<% content_for :title, "Theming — Shadwire" %>
<% content_for :description, "Shadwire's semantic tokens: where they live, what each one controls, how to change your brand colour, and how to keep your edits across updates." %>
<% content_for :head do %>
  <%= tag.style rouge_stylesheet %>
<% end %>

<article class="space-y-12">
  <%= render "docs/header",
        title: "Theming",
        description: "Components take their colours from CSS variables. To change the theme, you edit those variables and leave the components alone." %>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Where the tokens live") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      In <%= docs_code("vendor/shadwire/shadwire.css") %>. <%= docs_code("shadwire init") %>
      installs it and imports it in your app's Tailwind entrypoint, whose path
      <%= docs_code("bin/shadwire status --json") %> shows under <%= docs_code("tailwind.css") %>.
    </p>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">The file has three layers:</p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:layers], language: :css) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("@theme inline") %> turns <%= docs_code("--primary") %> into the
      <%= docs_code("bg-primary") %> and <%= docs_code("text-primary") %> utilities. The colours
      are written in OKLCH.
    </p>

    <%= render layout: "docs/callout", locals: { icon: "info", title: "Edit :root and .dark, never @theme inline" } do %>
      The <code>@theme inline</code> block only maps each variable to a Tailwind utility. The
      colours themselves are set in the <code>:root</code> (light) and <code>.dark</code> blocks.
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("The tokens") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Each background token has a <%= docs_code("-foreground") %> token for the text on top of
      it. Use the two together, as in <%= docs_code("bg-muted text-muted-foreground") %>, rather
      than <%= docs_code("bg-muted") %> with the default text colour.
    </p>

    <%= render "docs/table",
          headers: [ "Token", "Controls", "Used by" ],
          rows: @tokens.map { |token| [ docs_code(token[:token]), token[:controls], token[:used_by] ] } %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Changing the brand colour") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Set <%= docs_code("--primary") %> and <%= docs_code("--primary-foreground") %> in both
      blocks, and every component that uses <%= docs_code("bg-primary") %> picks up the new
      colour.
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:brand], language: :css) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Choose a foreground with enough contrast against the new primary, and check it in both
      light and dark mode.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Adding a token") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Declare the variable in both blocks and map it in <%= docs_code("@theme inline") %> so
      Tailwind generates the utilities:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:new_token], language: :css) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      After that, <%= docs_code("bg-success") %> and
      <%= docs_code("text-success-foreground") %> work like any other token.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Corner radius") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("radius-sm") %>, <%= docs_code("radius-md") %> and
      <%= docs_code("radius-lg") %> are all derived from <%= docs_code("--radius") %>, so
      changing it changes the corners of cards, inputs, buttons and popovers in one go.
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:radius], language: :css) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Adding a variant") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Variants are frozen Ruby hashes in the component's class. The file is yours, so adding a
      variant means adding a key:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:variant], language: :ruby) %>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:variant_usage]) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      This counts as a local edit: <%= docs_code("bin/shadwire diff") %> will list the file as
      <%= docs_code("modified") %>, and <%= docs_code("bin/shadwire update") %> would overwrite it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Keeping your edits") %>

    <ol class="ml-4 list-decimal space-y-2 leading-7 text-muted-foreground marker:font-medium marker:text-foreground">
      <li><%= docs_code("bin/shadwire diff <name>") %>: see what you changed.</li>
      <li>
        <%= docs_code("bin/shadwire update <name>") %> <strong class="font-semibold text-foreground">without</strong>
        <%= docs_code("--yes") %>: it asks about each modified file and shows the diff.
      </li>
      <li>Put your edits back on top of the new version.</li>
    </ol>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:keep_edits], language: :shell) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Where you can, add things (a new variant, a new token) instead of rewriting existing
      classes. Additions are much easier to carry over when you update.
    </p>
  </section>
</article>
